<<~ ahu #/tension>>

! Blob Carriage ~ a scout against the binaries tension

''Register: Model-Standing.'' A scout read the house whole, then the field whole; every house claim names a file and a line, every field claim names a URL and quotes the page. A claim without either names itself as inference.

''The operator's words, verbatim (quote license):''

> "Binaries serves as an interesting problem, we have some tensions around what 'rides the wiki as a tiddler with a type' and what 'rides the wiki as a tiddler pointing to a CAS/CID blob'. Needs research/best-practices/prior-art/golden-principles/pattern-integrity-rhymes. Hold that we may want 'live streams' at some point, along with packages like gameart.net (whole vector collections), access to online photo galleries if connected (MidJourney, user/operator's Google/Apple/Etc photos they authorize)."

Two shapes stand in the tension:

* ''TYPED TIDDLER'' — the bytes ride the tiddler itself (`text` holds base64 for a binary type, utf8 for a text type) and the `type` field says how to read them; the tiddler carries body AND identity in one record.
* ''POINTER TIDDLER'' — the tiddler carries a ''reference'' (`_canonical_uri`, a CID, a stream source) and the bytes live on a plane the reference names; the tiddler carries identity, type and size, and the plane carries the body.

The field adds a third the operator's held cases force into the open: a ''SOURCE TIDDLER'' — a pointer whose referent never rests as bytes at all (a live stream, an expiring gallery lease). #/held-cases names it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/as-built>>

!! As built ~ what the house does today, by size and kind

''The threshold law, two floors and one wall'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/content-handle.ts`):

| floor | value | what fires | file:line |
|---|---|---|---|
| ''hard inline wall'' | `SKINNY_CARRIER_THRESHOLD = 1 MiB` | a body past it MUST leave the CRDT or the ingest faults — "a verb rides a reference, never a body" | `content-handle.ts:35`, `:41-43`; the fault at `packages/lararium-tw5/src/action-handler.ts:692-693`, `:833-834` |
| ''opt-in-CAD backstop'' | `CAS_BACKSTOP_SIZE = 64 KiB` | an UN-flagged body goes skinny when its media family reads `image/` · `audio/` · `video/` · `application/octet-stream` (size-blind), OR it runs past 64 KiB and does not read `text/` | `content-handle.ts:50`, `casBackstopFires` `:80-92` |
| ''the flag'' | `_lar_cas: yes` | FLAG-PRIMARY: the operator's flag elects a skinny handle before any size reads | `packages/lares-cli/src/cas-stage.ts:68-77`; the writer `packages/lares-cli/src/tag-blobs.ts:1-30` (`lares ingest --tag-blobs`) |
| ''the automerge wall'' | ~2^24 chars per scalar string | the wall that felled the seed (#51, the 16 MB Twain) — "every peer that syncs the doc pays the whole weight" | `cas-stage.ts:9-11`; `content-handle.ts:4-6`; <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/content-resolution#/the-birth">> |

The canon names a further north the code holds in reserve: "big-on-fs, small-inline (~16KiB, Iroh)" (`content-resolution.mem:111`), with the 1 MiB wall "held generous … until the read-side lazyLoad resolver lands" (`content-handle.ts:31-33`). That resolver HAS landed (`packages/lararium-tw5/src/lazy-resolver.ts:51-104`, installed at `sovereign-kernel.ts:424`), so the generosity clause has expired and the threshold stands unrevisited — #/questions carries it.

''By kind, what the island writes:''

# ''A small text carrier (a meme, a `.md`, a `.tid`)'' rides INLINE — `mediaTypeFromExt` reads `.mem`/`.tid`/`.json`/`.multids` utf8-clean → `text/` → inline, "a bundle is inline by nature" (`content-handle.ts:61-73`).
# ''Any image / audio / video, at ANY size'' goes SKINNY: the backstop fires on the family alone (`content-handle.ts:80-92`). The CLI reads the file, fails the utf8 round-trip, and rides base64 (`packages/lares-cli/src/ingest-core.ts:82-87`); `stageBodyToCas` hashes `utf8Bytes(text)` — ''the CID keys the base64 STRING, never the file's raw bytes'' (`cas-stage.ts:20-23`, `:78-84`). The island lands `skinnyHandleTiddler` (`content-handle.ts:147-165`): `_is_skinny: yes` · `_canonical_uri: lar:///ha.ka.ba/cid/<hex>` · `_integrity: ni:///sha-256;…` · `textCid` · `size` · `type` (TW5's own field, from `$tw.config.contentTypeInfo`) · `_source_ext` — and ''no `text`''.
# ''A text carrier past 64 KiB that does not read `text/`'' (rare — `application/*` text) goes skinny by size; a `text/*` body stays inline up to the 1 MiB wall, then faults unless flagged.
# ''An oversized un-flagged body'' still STAGES for transport (never a giant inline arg) but lands `skinny: false`, so the island faults it rather than materialize it (`cas-stage.ts:70-74`).

''The two CAS tiers'' (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/content-resolution#/cad-storage">>):

* ''`cid/` — the KIND-plane'' holding bytes keyed by hash. The runtime store roots off vessel STATE, never the corpus: `larCasDir()` = `LAR_CAS` else `<vessel-state>/cas` — "its blobs rebuild from the `bags/` carriers on each seed, so they stay OUT of the tracked tree" (`packages/lares-cli/src/env.ts:96-104`); the node's `casDirForStorage` / `readCasBlobFromFs` (`packages/lararium-node/src/node-cas.ts:23`, `:86`); the genesis CAS (`genesis/cas/<cid>`, `packages/lararium-mesh/src/genesis-doc.ts:487-489`) keyed `cidV1Sha256` = `0x01 0x55 0x12 0x20` + digest, base32 (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/crypto.ts:249-263` — raw codec, sha2-256, the same shape AT-proto blobs ride). The bulb serves the whole public boot CAS content-addressed, "a puller re-verifies `sha256(bytes) == cid` on every blob BEFORE it trusts a byte" (`packages/lararium-node/src/bulb.ts:15-18`).
* ''`@cad` — the content-addressed TIER'' whose addressing mode the PUBLICITY PLANE sets: public → plaintext `ni://` multihash on `crossroads`; private → `cid = BLAKE3(ciphertext)` on `@catalog`, "verify-cap ⊥ read-cap" (`content-resolution.mem:80-95`). The seal installer exists: `packages/lararium-node/src/ciphertext-cas-seal.ts:1-17` (fail-closed with no per-Nexus secret). `bodyIndexBagUri` / `assertBodyIndexTier` hold "map-tier = body-tier" (`content-handle.ts:117-145`). ''`cas-stage` = CLEARTEXT LOCAL vs `@cad` seal = CIPHERTEXT FEDERATION'' — the #51 root-cause ruling (Fix=B).

''The read side'' — `lazy-resolver.ts` answers TW5's own `lazyLoad` (fired at `TiddlyWiki5/core/modules/wiki.js:1531-1536` when `_is_skinny` stands and `getTiddlerText` runs), pulls `resolveByCid`, re-verifies `cid == sha256(bytes)`, splices `text` into the VM under the `_applying` echo-guard so the body "NEVER echoes back out to the CRDT" (`lazy-resolver.ts:12-18`); `skinnyCid` scheme-discriminates — a `lar:///…/cid/` resolves, an `http(s)://`/`data:` src "is left to the native/DOM path" (`lazy-resolver.ts:20-24`, `:39-48`; `cidFromUri` `packages/lararium-mesh/src/lar-uris.ts:179-183`).

''What the DOM sees — a measured gap.'' TW5's image widget reads `text` first, then `_canonical_uri`, and only in the ELSE branch "Just trigger loading of the tiddler" via `getTiddlerText` (`TiddlyWiki5/core/modules/widgets/image.js:61-90`). A skinny handle carries `_canonical_uri` = `lar:///ha.ka.ba/cid/<hex>`, so an `<img>` transcluding it takes the `_canonical_uri` branch (`image.js:75-86`) and sets `src="lar:///…"` — the lazyLoad branch at `:88-90` never fires for an image, and `lar:` fetches nothing by law. The lazy resolver rehydrates on `getTiddlerText` paths (a `{{transclusion}}` of text), never on the image widget's src path. Inference, unwitnessed by a test: ''a skinny image renders as a dead `<img src>` in the browser'' until either the handle drops `_canonical_uri` for the image family or a `lar:` → `blob:` URL shore lands. #/proposed-law answers it.

''The disk projection of each:''

| kind | island projection | stock server (same tiddler, filesystem plugin) | file:line |
|---|---|---|---|
| inline text carrier | whole carrier, `.mem` recompose; a native type as content + `.meta` | the same — `generateTiddlerFileInfo` parity | `packages/lararium-tw5/src/tw5-file-info.ts:174-190`; `TiddlyWiki5/core-server/filesystem.js:240-247` |
| inline binary (`base64` type) | the projector DECODES base64 to raw bytes and writes `photo.png` + `photo.png.meta` | the same — `fs.writeFile(..., typeInfo.encoding)` | `tw5-file-info.ts:37-40`, `:201-203`; `filesystem.js:449-454` |
| skinny handle | `text` STRIPPED; the handle alone as `photo.tid` (TW5 routes any `_canonical_uri` tiddler to `.tid`); the source `.png` sweeps as a straggler | keeps `photo.png` + `.meta` whole (it never made a handle) | `packages/lararium-tw5/src/meme-write.ts:95-110`; `tw5-file-info.ts:179-181`; `filesystem.js:242`; MEASURED `tests/e2e/nonmeme-parity.test.ts:176-190`, `:219-220` |

Parity-Warden's measure stands OPEN by its own words: "where a binary belongs under bags/ reads OPEN (the operator wants talk-story first)" (`nonmeme-parity.test.ts:177-181`). The disk-projection canon rules "one tiddler, one file, TW5's own format law" for every non-meme type (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/disk-projection#/projection-routing">>) and "RENDER, not copy" (`#/projection-law`); the skinny path honors the first and, for a binary, renders a pointer where the operator wrote a file.

''The pack model as built'' — a `.json` / `.multids` / plugin bundle decomposes in the VM; membership rides the `$:/config/OriginalTiddlerPaths` aside; a member edit shadows UP into `@working`; the pack file stays ONE file on disk and REPACK re-renders it whole (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/wiki-layer-ontology#/pack-refinements">>); four refinements stand owed there, the first being "One descriptor·kind … `{member content-DIGEST, kind, annotations}`".

''The residency verbs'' — ADD · COPY · MOVE · CLEAR · DROP · LOAD · INGEST, every ADD/COPY/MOVE/LOAD carrying a `changeId` (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/residency-actions.ts:12-28`, `:228`). No verb today PINs or UNPINs a blob; the canon names retention by tag — "a cap-lifetime-bound tag PINs, an untagged body CACHEs under LRU; GC runs mark-and-sweep from the tags (Iroh tags). Each island pins its own" (`content-resolution.mem:114-118`) — designed, unbuilt.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/prior-art>>

!! Prior art ~ one row per system

Every row reads: what rides INLINE · what it POINTS TO · how it SPELLS the pointer · how it ADDRESSES the bytes · how it GCs · where TYPE and SIZE ride · the threshold it names — then the URL and the page's own words.

''TiddlyWiki core — the TYPED tiddler.'' Inline: the bytes, base64 in `text` when the type registers `encoding: "base64"` (`TiddlyWiki5/boot/boot.js:553-563` `registerFileType(type,encoding,extension)`; `:2528-2537` png · jpeg · gif · webp · pdf · zip · heic) · points to: nothing · spelling: none · addressing: the TITLE · GC: none (a tiddler deletes) · type: the `type` field, and "binary" DERIVES from the registry, never from the bytes — `isBinaryTiddler` returns `contentTypeInfo.encoding === "base64"` (`core/modules/wiki.js:244-249`; `is[binary]` `core/modules/filters/is/binary.js:15-26`); size: not carried · threshold: none. Disk: `fs.readFileSync(filepath, typeInfo.encoding)` reads a binary straight into `text` as base64 (`boot.js:1941-1954`); `saveTiddlerToFile` writes the body with the type's encoding (base64 decodes to raw bytes) + `.meta` = `getFieldStringBlock({exclude: ["text","bag"]})` (`core-server/filesystem.js:445-454`). "Files containing single tiddlers can also have an auxiliary `.meta` file formatted as a sequence of name:value pairs" (https://tiddlywiki.com/#TiddlerFiles).

''TiddlyWiki core — `_canonical_uri`, the POINTER tiddler.'' Inline: none — "If the canonical URI is provided then the ''text'' field of the tiddler is ignored and so should be omitted" · points to: an external resource · spelling: `_canonical_uri` = "the URI of the image. The URI can be absolute or relative to the HTML document" · addressing: LOCATION · GC: none · type: the `type` field still says how to render (the image widget switches on `deserializerType`, `core/modules/widgets/image.js:75-85`; the data-URI helper hands back the canonical URI instead, `core/modules/utils/utils.js:888-894`); size: not carried · threshold: none; the cost the docs name: "using them breaks the single file pattern of TiddlyWiki" (https://tiddlywiki.com/#ExternalImages). The docs cover images only — no `ExternalText` page exists, and `Fields.multids:3` reads "`_canonical_uri`: The full URI of an external image tiddler". Disk: saved as a plain `.tid`, no blob written (`core-server/filesystem.js:242-249`); on spec load, `if("_canonical_uri" in fields) { text = ""; }` — 5.4.0 "skip reading file content when `_canonical_uri` is present" (`boot/boot.js:2035-2036`, #9341). Import: the `external-attachments` plugin mints it — "instead of importing binary files as self-contained tiddlers, they are imported as "skinny" tiddlers that reference the original file via the ''_canonical_uri'' field … it does mean that the wiki is no longer fully self-contained" (`plugins/tiddlywiki/external-attachments/readme.tid:5`), a relative-or-absolute `file://` path (`startup.js:28-40`, `UseAbsoluteForNonDescendents`), firing ONLY when `document.location.protocol === "file:"` and `$:/config/ExternalAttachments/Enable` reads `yes` (`startup.js:24-25`).

''TiddlyWiki core — `_is_skinny`, the LAZY tiddler.'' "Lazy loading refers to the technique of only embedding metadata about the tiddler (in other words all fields except the **text** field), and requesting the body from the server when required" (https://tiddlywiki.com/#LazyLoading). `_is_skinny`: "If present, indicates that the tiddler text field must be loaded from the server" (`core/language/en-GB/Fields.multids:45`); `getTiddlerText` fires `lazyLoad` and returns `null` = "being fetched" (`core/modules/wiki.js:1525-1538`); the syncer answers it unless `$:/config/SyncDisableLazyLoading` reads `yes` (`core/modules/syncer.js:136-140`) and "Only load the skinny version if we don't already have a fat version" (`syncer.js:654-680`); `getSkinnyTiddlers` fetches field-only JSON (`plugins/tiddlywiki/tiddlyweb/tiddlywebadaptor.js:173-176`). The skinny marker and the pointer stand as TWO fields in TW5 — a lazy body (`_is_skinny`, server-resolved) and an external body (`_canonical_uri`, browser-resolved) — and the image widget honors `_canonical_uri` BEFORE it fires the lazy path (`image.js:61-90`).

''`tiddlywiki.files` — the SIDECAR SPEC.'' A directory declares, per file, `isTiddlerFile` ("the file will be treated as a tiddler file and deserialised"), `isEditableFile` ("changes to the tiddler be saved back to the original file"), `fields` sourced from `filename` · `basename` · `filepath` · `extname` · `created` · `modified` with `prefix`/`suffix` applied to any sourced field (https://tiddlywiki.com/#tiddlywiki.files%20Files ; `boot/boot.js:2017-2101` `loadTiddlersFromSpecification`); a binary file reads base64 per the registry (`:2043`) and an editable file keeps its path for write-back (`:2100-2101`, `:2348-2359`). This IS a typed-tiddler ingest of a folder of files with the fields DECLARED beside them — the same shape as `.meta`, lifted to the directory.

''MultiWikiServer (MWS) — the POINTER tiddler the SERVER mints.'' Inline: a binary BELOW the limit rides base64 in the row; ABOVE it the row holds only `attachment_blob` ("null or the name of an attachment blob", `sql-tiddler-database.js:161` at TW5 `e3ce0537`) · points to: a content-hashed file store — `contentHash = sha256(text)`; `files/<hash>/data.<ext>` + `meta.json` (`modules/store/attachments.js:19-22`, `:62-66`) · spelling: outbound, a server-minted `_canonical_uri` = `/bags/{bag}/tiddlers/{title}/blob` with `text: undefined` (current repo `packages/mws/src/services/attachments.ts:46`, `:59-65`); inbound, the client's `_canonical_uri` is STRIPPED and the blob stored by hash (`:109-110`) · addressing: CONTENT in the store, LOCATION on the wire · GC: not specified · type: the row's `type`; size: the row · threshold: `$:/config/MultiWikiServer/AttachmentSizeLimit`, default `204800`, floor `100 * 1024`, applied ONLY when `isBinary` and `EnableAttachments` reads `yes` (`sql-tiddler-store.js:147-169`) (https://github.com/TiddlyWiki/MultiWikiServer ; https://github.com/TiddlyWiki/TiddlyWiki5/pull/7915). MWS reads as the house's own skinny handle with one difference the DOM cares about: ''the `_canonical_uri` it mints RESOLVES'' — a route the browser fetches — and it never persists the client's pointer.


''IPFS / IPLD.'' Inline: small bytes in a DAG node (`{"/": {"bytes": "…"}}`) · points to: other blocks by CID (chunk leaves, directory children) · spelling: `{"/": "<cid>"}` in DAG-JSON · addressing: CONTENT — a self-describing multihash + codec · GC: pin-based, "unpinned + not in MFS" sweeps under `repo gc`; recursive pins hold whole DAGs · type/size: the codec rides IN the CID (`raw` 0x55 vs `dag-pb`), `dag-pb` carries `filesize`/`blocksizes`, MIME rides NOWHERE · threshold: "Implementations SHOULD use 256 KiB (popular legacy size) or 1 MiB (modern maximum recommended) for newly created blocks"; "Single-block files SHOULD prefer the `raw` codec (0x55) over `dag-pb`"; "MUST be able to decode blocks up to 2 MiB" (https://specs.ipfs.tech/unixfs/). "A CID is a self-describing content-addressed identifier" (https://github.com/multiformats/cid). "Pinning gives you control over disk space and data retention … IPFS uses garbage collection to free disk space on your IPFS node by deleting data that it thinks is no longer needed" (https://docs.ipfs.tech/concepts/persistence/). Link spelling: https://ipld.io/specs/codecs/dag-json/spec/.

''AT Protocol blobs.'' Inline: none — a record holds only the ref object · points to: the PDS blob store · spelling: `{"$type":"blob","ref":{"$link":"<cid>"},"mimeType":"…","size":N}` · addressing: CONTENT — `ref` reads "CID reference to blob, with codec type `raw`" (sha-256, base32 `b…`) · GC: by REFERENCE — an upload lands in temporary storage; "Blobs which are in temporary storage should not be included in the `listBlob` output"; unreferenced blobs delete after a grace of "several hours" with "at least one hour a firm lower bound"; a record reference retains · type/size: `mimeType` (required, default `application/octet-stream`) and `size` ("length of blob in bytes") ride BESIDE the link, "verified metadata" the PDS returns from `uploadBlob` · threshold: no protocol number; per-lexicon `maxSize`/`accept`, per-server quotas (https://atproto.com/specs/data-model , https://atproto.com/specs/blob).

''Matrix media.'' Inline: none · points to: a homeserver content repository · spelling: `url: "mxc://<server-name>/<media-id>"` inside event content (`file` for encrypted) · addressing: LOCATION — a server-minted opaque id, the origin server named in the URI · GC: none in spec — "media retention and deletion policies are determined by individual server implementations" · type/size: `info.mimetype` / `info.size`, client-asserted · threshold: `GET /_matrix/client/v1/media/config` returns `m.upload.size`, "the maximum permitted upload size in bytes"; authenticated media (v1.11, MSC3916) moves download under `/_matrix/client/v1/media/download/{serverName}/{mediaId}` (https://spec.matrix.org/latest/client-server-api/#content-repository).

''git-LFS.'' Inline: the pointer text only · points to: the LFS object store (`.git/lfs/objects/<oid[0:2]>/<oid[2:4]>/<oid>` local, the batch API remote) · spelling: three key-value lines `version https://git-lfs.github.com/spec/v1` / `oid sha256:<hex>` / `size <bytes>` · addressing: CONTENT (sha256 hex) · GC: by reachability from refs (`git lfs prune`) · type: NOT carried (extension only); size: the `size` line · threshold: "Pointer files must be less than 1024 bytes in size, including any pointer extension lines"; selection by PATH PATTERN in `.gitattributes` (`*.mp3 filter=lfs -text`), never by byte count (https://github.com/git-lfs/git-lfs/blob/main/docs/spec.md).

''Automerge.'' Inline: bytes ARE permitted ("Byte arrays", `new Uint8Array([1,2,3])`) and every version stays in history · points to: nothing native — no blob-ref type; docs link docs by `automerge:<docid>` string · spelling: none for blobs · addressing: LOCATION (document id); storage chunks by change hash — StorageAdapter keys "are arrays of the form `[<document ID>, <chunk type>, <chunk identifier>]`" · GC: none — history immutable, compaction only folds · type/size: not carried · threshold: NONE STATED; the closest guidance reads "We suspect that an Automerge document is best suited to being a unit of collaboration between two people or a small group" (https://automerge.org/docs/cookbook/modeling-data/ , https://automerge.org/docs/reference/documents/ , https://automerge.org/docs/reference/under-the-hood/storage/). ''The field carries NO "keep blobs out of the doc" sentence'' — the house learned that law by OOM (#51), and the scalar-string wall stands as house measurement, not upstream doctrine.

''Solid.'' Inline: a non-RDF resource IS the bytes at its own URI · points to: by plain HTTP IRI from RDF triples · spelling: `<https://pod/…/x.png>` · addressing: LOCATION; containment via `ldp:contains` · GC: none — explicit DELETE; "When a contained resource is deleted, the server MUST also remove the corresponding containment triple" · type: `Content-Type` — "Server MUST generate a `Content-Type` header field in a message that contains content"; size: `stat:size` in the container description · threshold: none (https://solidproject.org/TR/protocol).

''Nostr NIP-94 / NIP-96 / Blossom.'' Inline: a metadata EVENT only (kind 1063) · points to: an HTTP file server (NIP-96) or any Blossom server · spelling: tags `["url", …]` + `["x", <sha256hex>]` + `["m", <mime>]` + `["size", …]` + `["dim", "WxH"]` + `["ox", <original sha256>]`; Blossom path `/<sha256>.<ext>` · addressing: NIP-94 = LOCATION + hash-attested; Blossom = CONTENT — "Blossom blobs are addressed by sha256", "The `GET /<sha256>` endpoint MUST return the contents of the blob" · GC: owner-initiated `DELETE /<sha256>` under NIP-98 auth; NIP-96 dedups uploads and a delete removes only the deleter's ownership · type/size: `m` / `size` tags; Blossom descriptor `type` ("falling back to `application/octet-stream`") + `size` + `uploaded` · threshold: per-server `plans.*.max_byte_size` (https://github.com/nostr-protocol/nips/blob/master/94.md , https://github.com/nostr-protocol/nips/blob/master/96.md , https://github.com/hzrd149/blossom/blob/master/buds/01.md). ''NIP-94 IS a tiddler-shaped pointer beside an out-of-band blob'', field for field.

''Willow.'' Inline: an Entry = metadata only · points to: the Payload by digest · spelling: typed fields `payload_digest` ("The result of applying hash_payload to the Payload") + `payload_length` ("The length of the Payload in bytes") · addressing: the ENTRY by location (namespace/subspace/path/timestamp), the PAYLOAD by content · GC: prefix pruning + newest-wins per path; a store may hold entries without payloads · type: not carried; size: `payload_length` · threshold: none; "When a peer sends an Entry, it can choose whether to immediately transmit the corresponding Payload as well" (https://willowprotocol.org/specs/data-model/index.html , https://willowprotocol.org/specs/sync/index.html). Willow states the split the house built: the entry syncs, the payload travels on request.

''Obsidian / Logseq.'' Inline: none — the file sits beside the notes on disk · points to: a vault-relative path · spelling: `![[image.png]]` (Obsidian, resolved by filename across the vault) / `![](../assets/name_<ts>_0.ext)` (Logseq) · addressing: LOCATION · GC: none — orphans persist · type: extension only; size: not carried · threshold: none; an extension allow-list gates what counts as an attachment ("Default location for new attachments": "Vault folder" · "In the folder specified below" · "Same folder as current file" · "In subfolder under current folder", https://obsidian.md/help/attachments ; https://obsidian.md/help/file-formats). This IS the stock-server shape: `photo.png` + `.meta` whole beside the notes.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/golden-principles>>

!! Golden principles ~ where the field converges

Each principle names the prior art that holds it and the house law that rhymes.

# ''Content-address the bytes.'' IPFS (CID) · AT-proto (`ref` raw CID) · git-LFS (`oid sha256`) · Blossom (`/<sha256>`) · Willow (`payload_digest`) · npm (`integrity` SRI) · SRI itself ("the URL locates, the digest binds"). Location-addressers (Matrix `mxc://`, Solid, Obsidian) pay for it with un-verifiable fetches and server-policy GC. ''House:'' the `cid/` plane, `cidUri` "The name IS the content hash" (`lar-uris.ts:170-177`), the bulb's verify-before-trust, `_integrity` ni://.
# ''The pointer carries type + size + hash — and the hash covers the RAW bytes.'' AT-proto (`mimeType` + `size` beside `$link`) · NIP-94 (`m` · `size` · `x`) · Blossom descriptor · git-LFS (`size`, hash; type absent — its known weakness) · Willow (`payload_length`, type absent). ''House:'' `skinnyHandleTiddler` carries all three (`content-handle.ts:147-165`) — BUT the hash covers the base64 string (`cas-stage.ts:20-23`), so a foreign verifier hashing `photo.png` disagrees with `_integrity`. #/pattern-integrities names the break.
# ''GC by reference count or by pin; never by policy alone.'' AT-proto (referenced-by-a-record retains, unreferenced sweeps after a grace) · IPFS (pins) · git-LFS (reachability from refs) · Willow (an entry references its payload). Matrix and Solid leave it to the server — the field's cautionary pair. ''House:'' "a cap-lifetime-bound tag PINs, an untagged body CACHEs under LRU … Each island pins its own" (`content-resolution.mem:114-118`), and the cap-tier keystone (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/cap-tier">>) — a pin reads as a cap with a lifetime.
# ''Never a secret in the pointer.'' Matrix's "leaked-URL sin fused may-talk, may-resolve, and may-read into one URL" (`content-resolution.mem:130-131`); authenticated media (v1.11) exists to undo it. OAuth: "Access token credentials … MUST be kept confidential in transit and at rest" (RFC 6749 §10.3). ''House:'' the three gates that never fuse (`#/resolve-by-cid`), the reach plane's "PUBLIC is refused by TYPE" (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/reach-plane#/the-truncated-chain">>).
# ''A stream is a reference to a SOURCE; a blob is a reference to BYTES.'' HLS: "Both are UTF-8 text files containing URIs and descriptive tags"; a live playlist lacks `EXT-X-ENDLIST` (RFC 8216). DASH: `MPD@type="dynamic"` for live, `static` once it becomes a bounded asset. NIP-53: `streaming` URL + `status` while live, a separate `recording` URL after. WebRTC: SDP describes endpoints, nothing rests. ''House:'' `lar:` "names; it does not fetch" (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/noosphere-boot#/lar-uri">>) — the house already holds a name-that-never-fetches; a source tiddler rides that law.
# ''Small inline, large by reference, under ONE threshold law.'' IPFS 256 KiB / 1 MiB chunks, 2 MiB decode max · Iroh ~16 KiB inline (canon) · git-LFS: by PATH PATTERN not byte count · AT-proto: no inline blob ever (the ref object is the only shape) · Matrix `m.upload.size` per server. The field splits two ways — by SIZE (IPFS, Iroh) or by KIND (git-LFS, AT-proto, the TW5 `encoding: "base64"` registry) — and the strongest systems pick ONE axis. ''House:'' today holds BOTH — a family backstop AND two size floors AND a flag (`content-handle.ts:35-92`). #/proposed-law collapses it.
# ''The wiki-side identity stays the tiddler title; the bytes' identity stays the CID.'' AT-proto (record URI vs blob CID) · Willow (entry path vs payload digest) · npm (name@version vs `integrity`) · content-resolution: "a stable logical handle … never the raw cid as identity … an indirection map (logical name → current cid)" (`content-resolution.mem:208-214`). ''House:'' the loci title `lar:///w.w.w/photo` names; `textCid` keys.
# ''Round-trip to disk keeps the file whole.'' Obsidian/Logseq (the file IS the attachment) · TW5 filesystem (`photo.png` + `.meta`, `filesystem.js:247`, `:449-454`) · git-LFS (the checkout smudges the pointer back into bytes; the pointer never appears in the working tree) · Solid (the resource is the bytes). ''House:'' "RENDER, not copy" and "one tiddler, one file, TW5's own format law" (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/disk-projection#/projection-law">>); the skinny path currently writes the pointer alone (`meme-write.ts:95-110`) — git-LFS's smudge/clean pair names the cure: pointer IN the store, bytes ON the disk.
# ''Read the declaration, never the path.'' TW5's `type` field + `contentTypeInfo` registry decide encoding (`boot.js:2533` `registerFileType("image/png","base64",".png")`); AT-proto's PDS VERIFIES `mimeType` at upload rather than trusting the client. ''House:'' `type` "IS the TW5 slot" (`content-handle.ts:110-112`); the CARRIER GRADIENT law (graceful parsing hides files — read the DECLARATION).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/pattern-integrities>>

!! Pattern integrities ~ the rhymes, and where each breaks

* ''`_canonical_uri` IS TW5's own pointer tiddler.'' The core reads it at parse time (`wiki.js:1091-1092`, `:1132`), the image widget emits it as `src` (`image.js:75-86`), the filesystem saves any `_canonical_uri` tiddler as a `.tid` (`filesystem.js:242`), and `external-attachments` mints it on import. ''Where it breaks:'' TW5 spells a LOCATION (an `http(s)://` or relative path the browser fetches); the house spells a NAME (`lar:///…/cid/<hex>`) nothing fetches. The image widget then emits a dead `src`. The rhyme holds for `text` transclusion (the `lazyLoad` branch) and breaks for `<img>`.
* ''The house's skinny handle IS an AT-proto blob ref.'' Both carry `{cid, mimeType, size}` beside a record, raw codec + sha-256, base32 CIDv1 (`crypto.ts:249-263` mints the identical prefix `0x01 0x55 0x12 0x20`). ''Where it breaks:'' AT-proto hashes the UPLOADED BYTES; the house hashes `utf8Bytes(base64(bytes))` (`cas-stage.ts:78-84`), so the same `photo.png` carries a different CID in each — and `_integrity` cannot verify the on-disk file. AT-proto GCs by reference; the house holds no PIN verb yet.
* ''The pack model IS an IPFS directory DAG.'' `OriginalTiddlerPaths` pairs member name with member; one pack file on disk, REPACK re-renders (`wiki-layer-ontology#/pack-refinements`); UnixFS: "every link in `PBNode.Links` is an entry (child) of the directory, and `PBNode.Links[].Name` gives you the name of that child", one root CID addressing the whole. ''Where it breaks:'' the DAG names children by CONTENT DIGEST; the aside names them by TITLE only — the owed "One descriptor·kind `{member content-DIGEST, kind, annotations}`" refinement closes exactly that gap. And a pack's members INLINE in the CRDT (a bundle "is inline by nature"), where a DAG's leaves stay blocks.
* ''The `.meta` sidecar IS the git-LFS pointer's fields.'' Both sit beside a file the human sees whole; both carry a small key-value block (`getFieldStringBlock({exclude: ["text","bag"]})`, `filesystem.js:454`; `oid`/`size`). ''Where it breaks:'' LFS's pointer REPLACES the bytes in the store while the working tree shows the bytes; `.meta` carries fields for a file whose bytes ALSO sit in the store as base64. The island's skinny projection inverts LFS — pointer on disk, bytes off it — the one shape neither LFS nor TW5 ships.
* ''The house's skinny handle IS MWS's attachment.'' Both split a binary past a limit into a hash-keyed file store + a bodyless row carrying `_canonical_uri` (`attachments.ts:59-65`; `content-handle.ts:147-165`); both hash the TEXT form (MWS `sha256(text)`, `attachments.js:62-66`; the house `utf8Bytes(text)`) — the same base64-keying, shared. ''Where it breaks:'' MWS mints `_canonical_uri` OUTBOUND as a route the browser fetches and STRIPS it inbound (`:109-110`) — the pointer never persists; the house persists a `lar:` name the browser cannot fetch. MWS thresholds by SIZE within the binary kind (`204800`, floor 100 KiB); the house fires on the kind alone.
* ''`@cad` ciphertext CAS IS Matrix authenticated media.'' Both undo the leaked-URL sin by gating the fetch. ''Where it breaks:'' Matrix gates by SESSION at a LOCATION (`mxc://` stays server-minted, the server decrypts nothing because it never encrypted); `@cad` addresses by `BLAKE3(ciphertext)` so an untrusted holder verifies and repairs BLIND — "verify-cap ⊥ read-cap" — and the node "never decrypts" (`content-resolution.mem:141-144`). Matrix's E2EE rooms come closer (`file` with keys in the event) but put the key IN the pointer, the exact fusion gate 3 refuses.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/held-cases>>

!! Held cases ~ live streams · gameart.net-scale packs · authorized galleries

''Live streams — a SOURCE tiddler.'' A stream never rests as bytes; HLS's playlist "containing URIs" that a live server "MUST make a new version of … available" (RFC 8216), DASH's `dynamic` MPD, NIP-53's `["streaming", "<url>"]` + `["status", "live"]` all spell a pointer to a SOURCE with a liveness state, and NIP-53 hands the post-hoc blob a SEPARATE `recording` URL. ''Shape:'' the third — a tiddler with `type` (the manifest's own, `application/vnd.apple.mpegurl` / `application/dash+xml`), a `_canonical_uri` naming the source, and NO hash (no bytes to hash); when the stream ends, a `recording` field points at a blob tiddler whose CID stands. ''House holds:'' `lar:` names-never-fetches, `skinnyCid` already leaves a non-cid `_canonical_uri` "to the native/DOM path" (`lazy-resolver.ts:44-47`) — so a source tiddler flows through the read side today untouched. ''House lacks:'' any transport for media (the island "holds no fetch capability", `action-handler.ts:660-663`), a `status` field convention, and the DPI ceiling makes a live source a web2 reach by definition. ''Law:'' a stream URL that carries a bearer token (a signed HLS URL) is a CREDENTIAL and rides the reach plane at ≤ CONTRACT, never a bag (`reach-plane#/the-truncated-chain`).

''Whole vector collections (gameart.net · Kenney "60,000+" assets in one 516 MB ZIP).'' ''Shape:'' a PACK of pointer tiddlers — one pack descriptor (the DAG root: name, license, member list as `{title, cid, type, size}`) + one skinny handle per member, bytes in `cid/`. The field converges: UnixFS directory (root CID over name→CID links, HAMT-sharded past one block), git submodule ("recording a commit as a directory entry", mode 160000 — a POINTER, where subtree copies in), npm (`resolved` tarball URL + `integrity` SRI). ''House holds:'' the pack model at ahu grain, `OriginalTiddlerPaths`, REPACK, and the owed content-DIGEST descriptor (`wiki-layer-ontology#/pack-refinements`). ''House lacks:'' 60,000 tiddlers in one CRDT doc reads as its own wall (a bundle "is inline by nature" — and `.svg` reads `image/svg+xml`, utf8-clean, so today it INLINES as text, not skinny; `mediaTypeFromExt` `content-handle.ts:56-73` lists no `.svg`); a pack-level pin (pin the root, the members follow — IPFS recursive pin); a lazy MEMBER list (HAMT). ''Law:'' a pack imports by ONE `LOAD` under one `changeId`; the descriptor carries the license; the members stay skinny; a whole-pack PIN is one cap.

''Authorized photo galleries (Google Photos · Apple Photos · Midjourney).'' The field speaks in leases: Google — "Base URLs remain active for 60 minutes … Instead of storing the returned media items, your application should store the IDs of the media items … the URLs included in the response expire" (https://developers.google.com/photos/library/guides/access-media-items); whole-library read scopes ended March 31 2025 and "use the new Google Photos Picker API" (https://developers.google.com/photos/support/updates). Apple — `PHAsset.localIdentifier` "A unique string that persistently identifies the object", device-local, resolved on fetch; an iCloud Link "Anyone with the link can view … automatically expire after 30 days" (https://support.apple.com/guide/icloud/share-photos-and-videos-with-an-icloud-link-mm93a9b98683/icloud). Midjourney — no API; "You may not use automated tools to access, interact with, or generate Assets through the Services" (Terms of Service, docs.midjourney.com — read via search snippet, verify on page). ''Shape:'' a POINTER tiddler whose identity rides the CID of the FETCHED bytes, never the URL — the URL expires, the id belongs to the far side, the bytes belong to the operator once fetched under their own authorization. A `_source_uri` (provenance — "source-uri carries provenance, not an address to dereference", `action-handler.ts:660-663`) keeps the far-side id; the CID keys the body in `cid/` at the tier the operator declares (a private photo → `@catalog`, ciphertext). ''House holds:'' the reach plane whole — BRIDGE (never present-as-actor), OUTWARD-ONLY refused for a READ bridge (this case READS inward, so the mailbox lock inverts: a gallery bridge pulls and never posts), compose-and-request ("A browser vessel holds NO reach credential … a node in the fleet holds"), the vault-reads-as-a-bag floored at CONTRACT, per-service manifest (`reach-plane#/the-service-manifest`), content-addressed receipts. ''House lacks:'' an inbound-fetch verb (LOAD takes carriers the GESTURE supplies; a gallery bridge would supply them from a node-side fetch leg), the OAuth refresh leg named OPEN in the canon ("the vault NON-PASSIVE"), and Midjourney entirely (no lawful door; the operator downloads by hand and LOADs — the pragmatic first path, mirroring "pasting an attenuated token"). ''Law:'' a token NEVER rides a tiddler in any content bag; the bridge sits BEHIND the island boundary — "any web2 bridge sits behind an island boundary; no web2 ontology enters the Lares stack proper" (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/field-collision">>, `:116`); a gallery item, once fetched, becomes a blob the house owns (CID identity) with a provenance field, so an expired URL costs a re-fetch, never an identity.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/proposed-law>>

!! Proposed law ~ one law, the fewest changes

''THE BLOB LAW (proposed, RFC-2119).'' A tiddler's `type` DECIDES its carriage; size DECIDES only the fault line.

# ''KIND, not size, picks the shape.'' A tiddler whose `type` registers `encoding: "base64"` in `$tw.config.contentTypeInfo` (TW5's own declaration, `boot.js:553-563`, `:2528-2537`; the core's own `isBinaryTiddler`, `wiki.js:244-249`) MUST ride as a POINTER TIDDLER; a tiddler whose type registers `utf8` MUST ride INLINE. This is git-LFS's path-pattern law and AT-proto's no-inline-blob law read through TW5's own registry — ONE axis, read from the declaration. The family backstop (`casBackstopFires`) becomes this rule; the 64 KiB floor and the `_lar_cas` flag retire as decision inputs (the flag MAY survive as an operator OVERRIDE forcing a text carrier skinny — Scenario A's `#source-text` case).
# ''Size stays a WALL, never a switch.'' A utf8 body past `SKINNY_CARRIER_THRESHOLD` MUST fault (as today, `action-handler.ts:692-693`) unless flagged; the operator sets the number (#/questions). The ~16 KiB Iroh north governs `@cad` CHUNKING (range-verify slices), never the tiddler shape.
# ''The pointer spelling — ONE tiddler carries both faces.'' A pointer tiddler MUST carry TW5's `type` (the media dialect), `size`, and `_integrity` (ni:// over the RAW bytes); it MUST carry `textCid` as the CAS key; it MUST carry `_is_skinny` so `getTiddlerText` lazy-loads. It SHOULD carry `_canonical_uri` ONLY when that URI resolves on the reader's plane: for a `text/*` skinny body the `lar:///…/cid/` name (the lazy path reads it); for an `image/*`/`audio/*`/`video/*` body the handle MUST NOT carry a `lar:` `_canonical_uri` until a `lar:`→`blob:` src shore exists (else `image.js:75-86` emits a dead src) — the island MAY mint a per-session `blob:` object-URL into the VM tiddler's `_canonical_uri` at lazy-resolve time, MWS's own move (mint outbound, strip inbound, `attachments.ts:59-65`, `:109-110`), never persisted to the CRDT or the disk. Two TW5 laws force the omission from the persisted record: the filesystem routes ANY `_canonical_uri` tiddler to a bodyless `.tid` (`core-server/filesystem.js:242`), and the spec loader BLANKS the text of any `_canonical_uri` tiddler (`boot/boot.js:2035-2036`) — a persisted `lar:` pointer on a binary breaks the whole-file round trip on both doors. A SOURCE tiddler (a stream, a far-side gallery item before fetch) carries `_canonical_uri` = the source and NO `textCid`/`_integrity`. ''Identity:'' the wiki-side identity stays the TITLE; the bytes' identity stays the CID; `_source_uri` carries provenance (a far-side id, a gallery URL) and never resolves.
# ''The CID MUST hash the RAW bytes.'' `stageBodyToCas` MUST decode a `binary: true` carrier from base64 before hashing and storing (`cas-stage.ts:78-84`); `resolveByCid` returns raw bytes; the lazy resolver re-encodes base64 for the VM's `text` (TW5's own expectation for a base64 type). Then `_integrity` verifies `photo.png` on disk, the bulb's verify law holds for every blob, and the AT-proto rhyme stops breaking.
# ''The disk projection writes the WHOLE FILE beside a `.meta` — git-LFS's smudge.'' For a pointer tiddler whose bytes the local `cid/` holds, the projector MUST write `photo.png` (raw bytes) + `photo.png.meta` (the fields, `text` excluded, `_is_skinny`/`textCid`/`_integrity` INCLUDED as ordinary fields) — the stock server's own shape (`filesystem.js:247`, `:449-454`) — and MUST NOT write `photo.tid`. Where `cid/` lacks the bytes (PENDING), the projector MUST write the `.meta` alone and no content file, and MUST NOT sweep a `.png` it did not write. The ingest side reads `photo.png` + `.meta`, hashes, and lands the pointer — the clean filter. Under this law "a `.png` under `bags/`" reads as CANON with the bytes ON DISK and the CRDT holding the pointer — disk projects thought "in its native grain" (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/disk-projection#/granularity">>).
# ''Parity with a stock server.'' A stock server with the plugin reads the SAME `photo.png` + `photo.png.meta` and loads a base64 TYPED tiddler (it knows no CAS); it saves it back identically. The `.meta` fields `_is_skinny`/`textCid`/`_integrity` ride through it inert (a stock TW5 `_is_skinny` tiddler WITH `text` reads its text — `wiki.js:1531-1534` checks the field, the syncer only lazy-loads a bodyless one; INFERENCE, witness owed). So the SAME two files stand on both doors; the doors differ only in where the bytes rest at runtime. Parity holds on disk, diverges by design in the CRDT.
# ''GC and PIN ride the residency verbs and the cap-tier.'' A blob is RETAINED while any tiddler in any locally-held bag references its CID (AT-proto's rule) OR a PIN cap with a lifetime names it (Iroh's tag); an unreferenced, unpinned blob MAY sweep under LRU after a grace. `DROP <bag>` releases that bag's references; a pack PIN pins the root descriptor's members recursively. A blob's TIER follows the bag holding its pointer (map-tier = body-tier, `content-handle.ts:117-145`) and a declared tier only tightens (`cap-tier#/keystone`).

Fewest changes: (i) decode-before-hash in `cas-stage.ts`; (ii) the projector's skinny branch in `meme-write.ts:95-110` writes content + `.meta` instead of `.tid`; (iii) `skinnyHandleTiddler` omits the `lar:` `_canonical_uri` for the base64 families; (iv) `casBackstopFires` reads `contentTypeInfo.encoding` via the receive side's `$tw` (or `mediaTypeFromExt` gains `.svg` → `image/svg+xml` and the utf8 rule keeps it inline — a choice the operator makes). Nothing else moves.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/questions>>

!! Questions ~ only the operator answers

# ''The threshold number.'' 1 MiB stands "held generous … until the read-side lazyLoad resolver lands" (`content-handle.ts:31-33`) — the resolver landed. Does the wall drop toward the 256 KiB / 1 MiB IPFS chunk band, the ~16 KiB Iroh inline north, or stay? Under #/proposed-law the number gates only utf8 faults.
# ''Does a `.png` under `bags/` count as canon?'' RULED 2026-09-11 (operator, verbatim, quote license): "a .png under bags/ with a .meta reads canon (but we should have the path to residency move it to the other store styles)." So: YES, with its `.meta` beside it — the git-LFS shape of #/proposed-law clause 5 — and the residency verbs (`act MOVE`/`COPY`) MUST carry it between store styles (inline typed · skinny pointer · another tier's CAS) as they carry any carrier between bags. The remaining questions decide HOW the bytes rest at runtime, never whether the file is canon.
# ''Stock-server parity for binaries — a goal or a non-goal?'' If a goal, the projector MUST write content + `.meta` (law 5-6). If a non-goal, `photo.tid` stands and the island's disk reads as its own dialect.
# ''Which plane holds the gallery bridge?'' The reach plane names OUTWARD posting; a gallery bridge READS inward. Does it ride the same vault-as-a-bag floored at CONTRACT with an inverted mailbox lock, or does an inbound bridge earn its own plane? And does a fetched gallery photo land at `@catalog` (ciphertext) by default?
# ''SVG and the vector collections.'' `image/svg+xml` reads utf8-clean → inline today. A 60,000-member vector pack inlines 60,000 text tiddlers in one doc. Does KIND (`image/`) or ENCODING (`utf8`) win for SVG?
# ''The 64 KiB floor and the `_lar_cas` flag'' — retire, or keep the flag as the Scenario-A override alone?

<<~/ahu>>

